# S Language — Full Product Strategy & Blueprint
# Goal: Cover gaps → load-ready product → public production path
# Version: Blueprint 1.0 · Base: S 3.5.0 · Date: 2026-10-19

==============================================================================
0. VISION (one sentence)
==============================================================================
S becomes a trustworthy offline-first language people use to build real
CLI tools, sealed apps, small backends, and (phase by phase) web surfaces —
without pretending to replace Kotlin/JS on day one.

Success metric (12 months):
  - Strangers install in < 2 minutes
  - 1000+ `s run` successful sessions / month (or equivalent)
  - At least 3 real projects built by non-authors
  - Zero critical unrepaired security issues > 30 days

==============================================================================
1. CURRENT BASELINE (truth)
==============================================================================
HAVE:
  - Core language (fn, loops, class, opt, pipe, when, async surface)
  - CLI: run check test new build release hub doctor repl vm log
  - Shield .sbx, LSP basics, 123 tests, Waves 1–4 packaging docs
  - HTTP client builtins, JSON, hash, time

HAVE NOT (product blockers for "load"):
  - Public hosted install + website live
  - Web UI / HTML generation framework
  - Strong static types
  - Production concurrency + fast runtime
  - Real Android on-device CI
  - Package ecosystem with users
  - Observability under multi-user load

==============================================================================
2. PRODUCT DEFINITION — what users can BUILD (phased)
==============================================================================
TIER A — NOW → +6 weeks (must ship)
  A1. CLI tools & automation scripts
  A2. Data scripts (JSON/HTTP clients)
  A3. Learning / course materials
  A4. Sealed offline utilities (.sbx)

TIER B — +2–4 months
  B1. Small HTTP API servers (request → response)
  B2. Static site generators (S → HTML files)
  B3. Backend workers / cron-style jobs
  B4. Hub packages published by community

TIER C — +4–9 months
  C1. Simple interactive web apps (forms + server)
  C2. Optional faster runtime path
  C3. Android demo apps on device (not full Play ecosystem)
  C4. Paid support option (if demand)

OUT OF SCOPE YEAR-1:
  - Full SPA framework competing with React
  - AAA games
  - Drop-in Kotlin Android replacement

==============================================================================
3. GAP → FEATURE MAP (every deficiency covered)
==============================================================================
GAP                         | STRATEGY WORKSTREAM        | OUTPUT
----------------------------|----------------------------|------------------
No public website host      | W-SITE                     | Live docs + install
Cannot "build websites"     | W-WEB                      | s.html + static gen
Weak types                  | W-TYPES                    | Checker v2
Slow / Python-bound         | W-SPEED                    | SVM+opt + docs
Thin stdlib                 | W-STDLIB                   | Frozen + expanded API
Weak concurrency            | W-ASYNC                    | Structured tasks
Android incomplete          | W-MOBILE                   | Device smoke path
No load story               | W-LOAD                     | Bench + limits
No packages community       | W-HUB                      | Remote registry
Support unclear             | W-OPS                      | SLA tiers optional
Security claims risk        | W-SEC                      | Audit cadence
Privacy/telemetry           | W-OPS                      | PRIVACY kept honest

==============================================================================
4. WORKSTREAM BLUEPRINTS (step-by-step detail)
==============================================================================

--------------------------------------------------------------------------
W-SITE — Public presence (2 weeks)
--------------------------------------------------------------------------
Step 1: Choose host (GitHub Pages / Cloudflare Pages / Netlify)
Step 2: Deploy `website/` as root; custom domain optional
Step 3: README badges → CI status, version, license
Step 4: Download page: install.sh + zip of release tag
Step 5: Analytics only if consent (default off)
Exit: stranger opens URL, installs, runs hello in < 5 min

--------------------------------------------------------------------------
W-WEB — "S se website" path (6–10 weeks)
--------------------------------------------------------------------------
Phase W-WEB-1 (static pages) — 2 weeks
  Step 1: Builtin or module `html_escape`, `html_tag`
  Step 2: `s html build site/` reads `.s` templates → `.html` files
  Step 3: Example: blog list + about page generated from S
  Step 4: Tests for escape (XSS baseline)
  Step 5: Docs: "Static sites with S"

Phase W-WEB-2 (mini server) — 3–4 weeks
  Step 1: `s serve ./public --port 8080` static file server
  Step 2: Route table in S: path → fn(request) → response
  Step 3: JSON API example `/api/hello`
  Step 4: Concurrency model: sequential first, then thread pool optional
  Step 5: Load note: single-process limits documented

Phase W-WEB-3 (forms) — 2–3 weeks
  Step 1: Parse query + form body
  Step 2: Example contact form → log file
  Step 3: CSRF not full framework — document threat model
Exit: user can generate static site AND run small API in S

--------------------------------------------------------------------------
W-TYPES — Checker v2 (4–6 weeks parallel)
--------------------------------------------------------------------------
Step 1: Track types for let/var/fn params returns in scope table
Step 2: Call arity + arg type soft/hard errors
Step 3: list[T] / map[K,V] optional annotations
Step 4: Mode: s check --strict
Step 5: 50+ type tests
Exit: strict mode catches real bugs without blocking scripts

--------------------------------------------------------------------------
W-STDLIB — Expand + freeze (ongoing, 4 weeks burst)
--------------------------------------------------------------------------
Step 1: Freeze current API (already docs/14_STDLIB_API.sd)
Step 2: Add: path join, env get, regex match, csv split, date format
Step 3: Each function: example + test + doc line
Step 4: Versioned stdlib changelog
Exit: "batteries" enough for Tier A+B apps

--------------------------------------------------------------------------
W-SPEED — Performance (parallel, 6–8 weeks)
--------------------------------------------------------------------------
Step 1: Benchmark suite (fib, json, list map) baseline numbers
Step 2: SVM parity for hot ops (slice, while)
Step 3: Document "use vm when…"
Step 4: Optional: PyPy compatibility test
Step 5: Later research: transpile subset to C/Rust (Tier C)
Exit: published numbers + no false speed claims

--------------------------------------------------------------------------
W-ASYNC — Concurrency (4–6 weeks after server)
--------------------------------------------------------------------------
Step 1: Define task model (spawn already exists — formalize)
Step 2: Channel or queue primitive
Step 3: Server uses pool for requests
Step 4: Tests for race-free examples
Exit: small concurrent server demos

--------------------------------------------------------------------------
W-MOBILE — Android honesty path (8+ weeks, contributor-led)
--------------------------------------------------------------------------
Step 1: Keep sbridge init stable
Step 2: Document Gradle wiring with screenshots
Step 3: One reference app on emulator
Step 4: Optional CI with Android emulator (expensive — later)
Exit: "runs on emulator" not "Play Store ready"

--------------------------------------------------------------------------
W-HUB — Packages (4 weeks + ongoing)
--------------------------------------------------------------------------
Step 1: Remote index.json schema stable
Step 2: `s hub publish` local → tarball
Step 3: Host simple static registry (GitHub releases)
Step 4: First 5 official packages: html, httpx, jsonx, testkit, cli
Exit: third party can install one package

--------------------------------------------------------------------------
W-LOAD — Production under load (3–4 weeks)
--------------------------------------------------------------------------
Step 1: Define load profile: 10 / 100 concurrent scripted clients
Step 2: Bench `s serve` static + API
Step 3: Document max safe RPS on reference hardware
Step 4: Fail guidelines (when to put nginx in front)
Step 5: Health endpoint `/health`
Exit: load numbers + architecture advice

--------------------------------------------------------------------------
W-SEC / W-OPS — Security & operations (continuous)
--------------------------------------------------------------------------
Step 1: Quarterly Shield self-audit (docs/17)
Step 2: Dependency review (Python stdlib only where possible)
Step 3: SECURITY.md response process drill
Step 4: Support tiers in SUPPORT.md when first paid user appears
Step 5: No silent telemetry (PRIVACY.md)

==============================================================================
5. MASTER SEQUENCE (calendar — sequential + parallel)
==============================================================================

MONTH 1 — "Public + Static Web"
  Week 1: W-SITE live deploy + tag v3.5.x
  Week 2: W-WEB-1 html helpers + static generator MVP
  Week 3: W-STDLIB burst (regex, env, path)
  Week 4: W-TYPES start + polish errors; soft beta announce

MONTH 2 — "Small servers"
  Week 5–6: W-WEB-2 s serve + routes
  Week 7: W-LOAD baseline benches
  Week 8: W-HUB first packages + docs

MONTH 3 — "Hardening"
  Week 9–10: W-TYPES strict mode
  Week 11: W-SPEED SVM parity push
  Week 12: Official launch event (HN etc.) if metrics ok

MONTH 4–6 — "Tier B complete"
  W-WEB-3 forms, W-ASYNC, W-MOBILE emulator demo, more packages

MONTH 7–12 — "Tier C selective"
  Faster runtime research, optional paid support, audit if funded

==============================================================================
6. STEP-BY-STEP EXECUTION PROTOCOL (how we work each item)
==============================================================================
For EVERY feature:
  1. Spec (.sd) — behavior + non-goals
  2. Tests first or with code (s test green)
  3. Implement minimal vertical slice
  4. Example in examples/
  5. Doc line in stdlib or tutorial
  6. CHANGELOG entry
  7. Doctor/smoke still pass
  8. Only then mark checklist [x]

No feature ships without: test + example + doc.

==============================================================================
7. "WEBSITE WITH S" — explicit product answer
==============================================================================
Users WILL be able to:
  1. Generate multi-page static websites from S (Month 1–2)
  2. Serve static files + JSON APIs with `s serve` (Month 2)
  3. Handle simple forms to files/logs (Month 3–4)

Users will NOT (year 1) get:
  - React-level SPA framework
  - Built-in fancy CSS framework (use normal CSS files)
  - Automatic scaling cloud

Architecture recommendation for production sites:
  [Browser] → [CDN/static] → optional [nginx] → [s serve API]

==============================================================================
8. RESOURCE PLAN
==============================================================================
Minimum (solo / small team):
  - 1 language owner
  - 1 docs/site owner (can be same person)
  - Community triage 2–3 hrs/week after launch

Tools:
  - GitHub + Actions (have)
  - Pages host
  - Optional Discord

Budget optional:
  - Domain
  - Formal audit only if commercial claims need it

==============================================================================
9. GO / NO-GO GATES
==============================================================================
GATE Soft beta:
  [ ] Public repo + LICENSE + CI green
  [ ] Live website install works on clean OS
  [ ] Static HTML generation demo published

GATE Official launch:
  [ ] s serve demo public
  [ ] 20+ external issues or stars OR 5 real user projects
  [ ] Load doc published
  [ ] Security process tested once

GATE "Product under load":
  [ ] Bench numbers public
  [ ] Health endpoint
  [ ] Failure mode docs (what breaks first)

==============================================================================
10. RISK REGISTER
==============================================================================
Risk: Overpromise web framework → mitigate: Tier language in README
Risk: Python speed complaints → mitigate: honest PERF docs + SVM path
Risk: Single maintainer burnout → mitigate: CONTRIBUTING + scope freeze
Risk: Shield marketing vs reality → mitigate: SECURITY.md exact claims
Risk: Scope creep Android → mitigate: emulator demo only until funded

